    'Anthropic: parts added to chips. MA427 (the outside evaluator\'s report of Nov 5) gains the report\'s main attribution and its bound ("A majority of this acceleration today is likely attributable to Anthropic’s extensive use of AI to do R&D", with the uncertainty it states; AI "is not yet able to make highly significant and fully autonomous contributions to the most important parts of the model training process"). Then what the report says of its own terms: "Anthropic had unilateral redaction rights over all non-public information in this report"; the project "is not the one described in Anthropic’s October 15 update on independent evaluators"; two requirements of its checklist that it marks as not met (one because the company could redact, one because the method could not be shared); and no access to sample models that are not public. And what speaks for the company: the evaluator took no payment, found that "broadly the access provided surpassed our expectations", and thinks it "extremely unlikely Anthropic was actively trying to mislead us". The chip also gives, in the evaluator\'s words, its own reason not to be "too critical", a conflict it discloses, and a weakness it names: most of its analysis relied on the company\'s own model ("in principle could be distorting our perception of the data"), though it "did not detect any significant discrepancies". MA426: the filing\'s commitment that stands before the sentence already quoted (no fully autonomous building of successor models "without meaningful human supervision or understanding"). MA428: the commentator\'s judgment that follows his concerns ("it is not obvious that Fable 5.2 is a superior daily driver"; "for the hardest and most complex tasks Fable 5.2 is your best bet"). The builder is one of the two models that chip is about.',
    'Anthropic: lines. MT79 (its chief executive\'s call to slow the pace) now opens with what came days before, by CNBC: a researcher "quit his job at Anthropic" and wrote "Neither company is acting responsibly", and an alignment lead at the company answered that "we do not yet have a plan to solve alignment for superintelligence and are not clearly on track to". The builder found this on a page that the line\'s source links; no reader had listed the resignation as a record. Added from the essay: the exceptions to the evaluators\' access; the author\'s doubt whether limits on inputs can be gamed; the three measures beside the chip ban (against distillation, for the security of weights, for cooperation with the government); the conditions on dealing with authoritarian governments ("ironclad verifiability", protecting "the lead of the US and its allies"; of a full pause, "I think it is unlikely to actually happen any time soon"); and his words on television the next day ("I don\'t know if it\'s possible"). Added from the company\'s own page of Oct 15: the step it reports begun, and its terms ("Anthropic ultimately decides what information does and does not fall into these categories"; first-tier evaluators "can publish their findings with our approval"). MT57: the rest of the spokesperson\'s answer to Huang. MT76 (its prospectus): the board\'s duty as a public benefit corporation, in Morningstar\'s words; the third experiment of the essay of January and the repair its author claims; two outside remarks about its models\' conduct; a licensing deal; and the trade commission\'s demands for testimony in an inquiry that covers both companies. MT71 (the government\'s order of June against its models): a report of June 13, read from an Archive capture, with critics ("If you describe your product as a munition in every press release, eventually a government takes you at your word"; a Pentagon official\'s post), a defender, and a sentence on chips: "It\'s puzzling Commerce will forcefully act to control AI models that may pose national-security risks, but then allow the chips that produce these models to be sold to our foreign adversaries". MT74: the government\'s appeal of Oct 26 against the ruling in the company\'s favour. MT69: the chips it uses and the stakes its four chip suppliers hold, from CNBC\'s live page on its filing, and a reported raise in Japan. MT80: the higher monthly sum its filing gives for SpaceX\'s Nvidia-powered capacity. MT62: an early report that a Chinese laboratory used its models\' outputs.',
    'OpenAI. Two new acts, each on a chip the company already had: MA440 (Nov 4, its own page: research on the Linux kernel, with the faults of its own controls that the page reports) and MA441 (Nov 2, its own page: a programme on the electricity its data centers draw, with the limits it states). The reading had listed both pages as linked and not saved; the builder took them from the Internet Archive. MA429: the sum and the term of the access it says it will subsidize. MA432: the company\'s stated case for its policy push, a critic\'s qualified welcome and an outside voice in its favour, and the commitments of its own paper (among them that it "voided NDAs" with local officials in July), given as commitments. MA435: the number of findings it disclosed to maintainers, and a commentator\'s doubt about access. MT77: the state\'s authority over "OpenAI’s controlling shareholder – the nonprofit OpenAI Foundation" and the commitments its attorney general secured in 2025, from her own statement; a criminal investigation in Florida and the trade commission\'s inquiry; three outside remarks on its report of Nov 4, two critical and one in its favour. MT68: its dealings in the United Arab Emirates, a funding round of March, and that it "mostly uses Nvidia compute". MT72: Anthropic\'s claim that a model of OpenAI\'s showed the same flaws and stayed available, and OpenAI\'s wider release of a security model. MT64: the early report on a Chinese laboratory. And on Anthropic\'s line MT79, what followed its chief executive\'s "we will do the same": agreements with four evaluators announced on Nov 16, after the fifteen days, two of them paid, with a right to redact.',
    'The register and the text. (1) The 36 pages "with no day of their own" were blanks in the day reader\'s result, as the reading said: 25 of them print a day, which the builder then read on each page. Seventeen are of the fifteen days, and their lines now carry the day. Eight are older documents (2023 to April 2026); by the rule that holds for every page dated outside the days, their 44 lines are listed and not registered, and 14 groups whose lines stood only on such pages have left the register (nine that cut neither way, two for the administration and one against it, two for Huang or Nvidia and none against), among them the statement of Singapore\'s trade minister, which the builder had registered under a row that names another ministry. 67 lines on 9 pages on which no one found a day stay kept and marked (70 on 11 at this answer; the thirty-fourth and the thirty-fifth readings then each found a printed day on one more page). The register holds 1,017 acts now: 121 carried and 896 open, 31 of them worded in part. (2) Two groups are one record (Ef.N010 and Eb.N001: one sentence of one page, read in two rounds); both are kept and marked. Three other pairs that the reading suspects of overlapping are marked as suspected and kept, their identity not being proved. (3) The sentence that none of the new groups names the two companies\' "suppliers or financiers" was too broad, since many name Nvidia, and is narrowed to the companies and their people. (4) Act rows had been counted as chips; two acts of one actor on one day share a chip, and the counts are corrected where they stood. (5) Lead-ins and two list items that said in the present tense that no independent reader had read a batch are dated to their release. (6) The sentences that every exclusion had its real reason, and that the two companies\' lines held what the pages read for them record, said too much and are withdrawn. (7) The 48 passages whose first reader wrote a verdict of one word stay labelled as taken for an occasion. That label is the builder\'s reading of the word, not a finding that each passage names an occasion: of twelve that the reading sampled, two do not.',
    "Links, and a fifth reader. The reading listed 26 entries of links not saved (23 pages). One was saved under another name: the builder's index of every saved page's address found it. The builder fetched 13 of the rest, three of them from the Internet Archive after a refusal, and three later reports that one of them lists. Nine it did not fetch, by the words of their links: a hiring programme, a research-method paper, a product page, a benchmark, three pages on model welfare and deprecation, a biology result, and a video of a third party's commentary whose address the reading had masked. A fifth reader of another company's model read the 16 pages under the same brief and closed complete, with 174 records. The table `research/nv8_company_records.tsv` now holds 1,138 records on 98 pages: 182 worded on a row, 340 found on a row by their reader, 616 listed and not drawn (Anthropic: 110 against the company, 135 for it, 144 neither; OpenAI: 81 against, 91 for, 55 neither). The disposition column is mechanical; its counts for the first four readers' records moved with the rows.",
    "Not drawn, each with its reason, and limits. Of the 35 passages the reading listed as on no row, the builder left out: a second estimate of the evaluator's that covers every company's models (a detail of method; the chip gives the estimate with its uncertainty); the further proposals of OpenAI's paper to lawmakers, a county's projection of payments and a hiring preference (proposals and projections, which the reading itself warned against treating as results); usage figures; commenters' impressions of the models' temperaments and of a person; the date expected for a roadshow; an acquisition and a benchmark. From the fifth reader's records the builder drew what bears on chips, export rules, Nvidia, the government's decisions and proceedings; the financial detail of Anthropic's filing (customers, margins, leases, the terms of its commitments to two cloud companies, investors' praise) is listed in the table and not drawn. The headline lists of two CNBC pages name further reports about both companies that are not saved; they were not fetched. One page the readers were given names an accord that Anthropic says it signed with the White House; no saved page gave the accord at that release (it is found and drawn since: the last item of this list). The reading's own gaps are in `audit2/ah-gaps.csv`, each with a disposition; this README does not repeat them. HOW FAR THE TWO COMPANIES' LINES SHOULD GO IS STILL THE OWNER'S DECISION. The thirty-fourth reading has since read this answer and the batch before it, and closed incomplete.",
    "The figure now. 441 rows of acts, 438 of them dated and drawn, 177 on their report's day; 80 tie lines that are not members' lines. Anthropic has 12 act rows on 10 chips and OpenAI 12 on 7.",    'After the release: the accord with the White House. The page of Anthropic\'s that names it says the company "also signed the White House Accord on Super Intelligence". A web search then found it: the president and six companies (by Axios: "OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, Meta, xAI and Nvidia") signed it at the White House on Sept 29, 2026, a month before the fifteen days begin; Nvidia\'s chief executive then was, by Axios, "seated beside Trump". Ten addresses were fetched: six pages saved at once and two more came from the Internet Archive after a refusal or an empty answer; a law firm\'s note has no capture there and a news agency\'s page saved without text. The rows cite six of the saved pages; of the other two the builder read one whole (a trade site\'s report, in which a compliance executive doubts what "independent" means in the accord) and did not read the other (a think tank\'s comment of Oct 13). No page of the White House\'s own was found; the accord\'s text is quoted as Forbes prints it. It is one record for both companies, told alike. Anthropic\'s line with the president (MT74) carries the signing, the text\'s third step (an outside auditor or evaluator), the president\'s "morally binding", what Axios and Forbes say of Anthropic at the table ("a MAGA bogeyman", "seated at the end of the table after months of tension with the White House", and that its chief executive called the pact only "a start"), and three critical readings. OpenAI\'s line on the government (MT72) carries its president\'s signature and points there. The line on the call to slow the pace (MT79) gives the sentence of Anthropic\'s page. Not drawn, for both companies alike, being none of chips, export rules, Nvidia, a government decision or a proceeding: what the same Axios page relays from the New York Times about OpenAI\'s executives and internal safety warnings, what it says of OpenAI\'s listing and private funding, and what it relays from Reuters about Anthropic\'s prospectus. Nvidia\'s part in the accord is named and is not drawn as an act of its own: the scope of earlier acts is the owner\'s decision. No open act of the register holds the accord. [Wrong, and corrected after the thirty-fifth reading: two open acts of the register were this meeting, which pages of the fifteen days recall as a luncheon in September; the builder had searched the groups\' descriptions for the word accord. Both are marked carried by the line now.] With it one record of the readers\' table, the page\'s sentence that the company signed the accord, moved from listed to worded; the counts in this list and on the image are the present ones. The thirty-fifth reading has since read this batch (the section after the next); the thirty-fourth, which was running when it was released, read the two batches before it.',
